\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Model dynamics at the baseline registry (Table~\ref {tab:params}), regenerated from the deposited code. Account amounts $B$ and $E$ are in gha; land areas $A_f,A_c,A_r$ are in ha; population $P_0$ is in cap. (a) Composition mask under frozen demand $E=0.825$ (restoration off, $A_{f0}=0.6$, $A_{c0}=1.5$, dimensionless $q_0=0.9$): biocapacity $B$ rises from $0.594$ to $0.825$ while capital land $A_c$ falls from $1.500$ to $0.808$ and fast land $A_f$ rises from $0.600$ to $0.923$; the shaded region marks the interval on which $\dot  B>0$ and $\dot  A_c<0$ hold simultaneously ($\approx 150$\,yr at numerical resolution; only the first 60\,yr are shown; $98\%$ of the rise in $B$ and $85\%$ of the fall in $A_c$ occur within the first 25\,yr). (b) The frozen-demand balance branch $B_{eq}(A_c)$ of Proposition~\ref {prop:ceiling}, strictly decreasing in $A_c$, with the verified balance points $A_c^*(E)=1.110,\,0.626,\,0.385,\,0.143,\,0.070$ at $E=0.70,\,0.90,\,1.00,\,1.10,\,1.13$; the branch meets the typed floor $A_c^{\min }=0.05$ at the demand ceiling $E_{\mathrm  {ceil}}\approx 1.138$, above which no admissible balance point exists and the capital book approaches the floor (shaded). (c) Endogenous-demand runs ($\tau _g=20$, $\tau _p=25$\,yr, $\chi _r=0.10$): the minimum capital land reached during a run is a continuous, monotone function of initial demand $P_0$ (hence $E_0=eP_0$) for each initial capital $A_{c0}$; the floor is approached asymptotically, and the floor-proximity thresholds of Table~\ref {tab:model} (triangles) are the operational points at which the floor is approached to within $10^{-6}$\,ha. There is no discontinuity in the outcome as a function of demand.}}{14}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:model}{{1}{14}{Model dynamics at the baseline registry (Table~\ref {tab:params}), regenerated from the deposited code. Account amounts $B$ and $E$ are in gha; land areas $A_f,A_c,A_r$ are in ha; population $P_0$ is in cap. (a) Composition mask under frozen demand $E=0.825$ (restoration off, $A_{f0}=0.6$, $A_{c0}=1.5$, dimensionless $q_0=0.9$): biocapacity $B$ rises from $0.594$ to $0.825$ while capital land $A_c$ falls from $1.500$ to $0.808$ and fast land $A_f$ rises from $0.600$ to $0.923$; the shaded region marks the interval on which $\dot B>0$ and $\dot A_c<0$ hold simultaneously ($\approx 150$\,yr at numerical resolution; only the first 60\,yr are shown; $98\%$ of the rise in $B$ and $85\%$ of the fall in $A_c$ occur within the first 25\,yr). (b) The frozen-demand balance branch $B_{eq}(A_c)$ of Proposition~\ref {prop:ceiling}, strictly decreasing in $A_c$, with the verified balance points $A_c^*(E)=1.110,\,0.626,\,0.385,\,0.143,\,0.070$ at $E=0.70,\,0.90,\,1.00,\,1.10,\,1.13$; the branch meets the typed floor $\Acmin =0.05$ at the demand ceiling $\Eceil \approx 1.138$, above which no admissible balance point exists and the capital book approaches the floor (shaded). (c) Endogenous-demand runs ($\tau _g=20$, $\tau _p=25$\,yr, $\chi _r=0.10$): the minimum capital land reached during a run is a continuous, monotone function of initial demand $P_0$ (hence $E_0=eP_0$) for each initial capital $A_{c0}$; the floor is approached asymptotically, and the floor-proximity thresholds of Table~\ref {tab:model} (triangles) are the operational points at which the floor is approached to within $10^{-6}$\,ha. There is no discontinuity in the outcome as a function of demand}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{eq:J}{{19}{15}{Balance point, demand ceiling and affine deficit-side dynamics}{equation.19}{}}
